WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.800
The universe is held together in such balance and harmony.

2
00:00:05.800 --> 00:00:11.140
Without a great power sustaining and governing it, everything would be in chaos.

3
00:00:11.960 --> 00:00:16.280
But we do not understand. So we can only turn to poetry.

4
00:00:17.920 --> 00:00:24.120
There are many mysteries in heaven and earth.

5
00:00:24.120 --> 00:00:29.980
The Chinese wrote songs of wonder—simple poems.

6
00:00:29.980 --> 00:00:37.840
You know that poem: “Heaven and earth are empty; life is fleeting among them.”

7
00:00:37.840 --> 00:00:45.640
The sun and moon rise and set—who are we working so hard for?

8
00:00:45.640 --> 00:00:55.360
Gold is empty, silver is empty. Who can hold them after death?

9
00:00:56.180 --> 00:01:08.520
Think about it: what becomes of the wealth of so many rich people? Money cannot fill the emptiness.

10
00:01:09.980 --> 00:01:26.080
Fields, houses, land—all pass away. Even real estate cannot give lasting meaning.

11
00:01:27.400 --> 00:01:37.300
I have seen many wealthy people leave this world with empty hands.

12
00:01:37.300 --> 00:01:47.000
The richest was Solomon in the Bible. His wealth was astonishing, and his wisdom unmatched.

13
00:01:47.000 --> 00:02:00.460
Yet he wrote Ecclesiastes: the eye is never satisfied with seeing, nor the ear filled with hearing. Much study wearies the body.

14
00:02:00.460 --> 00:02:07.720
He said, “Vanity of vanities; all is vanity under the sun, like chasing the wind.”

15
00:02:07.720 --> 00:02:14.320
It seemed he had enjoyed everything and had everything he could want.

16
00:02:14.320 --> 00:02:23.780
George Bernard Shaw said life has two great tragedies. The first is not getting what you want.

17
00:02:25.200 --> 00:02:32.560
The second great tragedy is getting what you want.

18
00:02:32.560 --> 00:02:39.500
Many long to enter a certain school, but never get in—and regret it for life.

19
00:02:39.500 --> 00:02:49.180
I have asked people at many top universities: once you get in, then what?

20
00:02:49.180 --> 00:02:56.420
The excitement lasts only a few days. Then it is, “So what?” That is all.

21
00:02:56.420 --> 00:03:04.300
Shaw put it humorously, yet truthfully: not getting what you want is painful.

22
00:03:04.300 --> 00:03:12.160
Life has two great tragedies. The second is getting what you want.

23
00:03:12.160 --> 00:03:20.020
You hope to marry your prince, but he marries someone else, and you feel regret.

24
00:03:20.020 --> 00:03:31.420
Then perhaps you do marry him. In time, you discover the truth: a white horse outside, a dark heart within.

25
00:03:31.960 --> 00:03:36.560
People are hard to predict.
